📘 AutomaticBackupScript.ps1 — Technical Documentation
📄 Overview

AutomaticBackupScript.ps1 is a PowerShell automation tool designed to safely extract and back up data from a user profile on a Windows device. The script runs unattended, identifies the appropriate profile, copies essential directories, generates detailed logs, and optionally produces an HTML/PDF report.

This documentation describes the script’s internal logic, features, design decisions, and operational guidance.

🎯 Purpose

This script solves common IT operational challenges:
Automating offboarding workstation data retrieval
Standardizing backup processes across varying device states
Reducing human error during manual file collection
Providing structured, timestamped backup archives
Creating audit-ready logs and reports
It is ideal for automation engineering, IT asset recovery, IAM offboarding workflows, or endpoint management teams.

⚙ Architecture & Workflow
🔍 Step 1 — Identify Last Logged-In User

The script attempts multiple registry queries to safely determine the most recent interactive user:

HKLM:\...\Authentication\LogonUI\LastLoggedOnSAMUser
HKLM:\...\Authentication\LogonUI\LastLoggedOnUser
As fallback: Win32_ComputerSystem.UserName
Once found, it resolves the user’s profile directory under C:\Users.

📂 Step 2 — Build Backup Folder Structure

A new folder is created using the format:

<Username>_<yyyyMMdd_HHmmss>

Example:

JohnDoe_20250115_094233\

If the network share is unreachable, the script automatically switches to:

C:\ProgramData\UserBackup_Fallback

📁 Step 3 — Select Directories to Back Up

The script dynamically determines which folders exist:

Desktop
Documents
Pictures
OneDrive (auto-detected — works for personal & business tenants)
Empty directories are skipped to reduce noise in the backup.

📦 Step 4 — Index All Files Before Copying

Instead of copying files one by one blindly, the script:

Scans all selected directories
Builds a collection of file & folder objects
Groups them by root folder
Calculates item counts for progress bars

This enables:

✔ multi-stage progress
✔ accurate percent tracking
✔ dry-run testing

📤 Step 5 — Copy Files With Safe Error Handling

For each item:

Folders → created automatically

Files → copied using Copy-Item -ErrorAction Stop

If a file is locked by another process:

Skipped (in use): C:\Users\User\Documents\example.docx

Other errors are logged but do not stop execution.

🧪 Dry Run Mode

If executed as:

.\AutomaticBackupScript.ps1 -DryRun


No files are copied. Instead, the script logs:

DRYRUN: Would copy C:\Users\User\Desktop\file.txt → \\backup\JohnDoe_2025...

This is essential for testing in production environments.

📊 Progress Visualization

The script uses two progress bars:

[###########------] Overall backup progress
    [#####-------] Current folder progress

This mirrors the structure of enterprise-grade automation tools.

📝 Logging System

A detailed log file is created:

BackupLog_<timestamp>.txt

Contains:

Start & end time
User being backed up
Destination path
All file operations
All errors or skipped items
Cancellation events (Ctrl + C)

Example Log Snippet:
[2025-01-15 09:42:01] User profile: C:\Users\JohnDoe
[2025-01-15 09:42:02] Queued 233 items from Documents
[2025-01-15 09:42:10] Copied: C:\Users\JohnDoe\Documents\Report.xlsx
[2025-01-15 09:43:55] Backup completed. Files: 188, Folders: 45

📑 Report Generation (HTML + PDF)

After the backup completes, the script builds:

BackupReport_<timestamp>.html
BackupReport_<timestamp>.pdf (if Microsoft Edge exists)

The report includes:

Device name
Username
Destination path
Embedded full log

Why Edge?
Edge supports headless printing to PDF, enabling the script to generate PDFs without external dependencies.

⚠ Error Handling & Safety Controls
Built-in protections:

Ctrl + C interception — logs cancellation without corrupting backup
Network path validation — fallback to local storage
Graceful handling of locked files
Try/catch blocks across all critical operations
All failures are written to the log and report.

🧩 Parameters
Parameter	Description
-NetworkPath	Overrides the default backup share
-DryRun	Simulates the backup without copying files

Example:

.\AutomaticBackupScript.ps1 -NetworkPath "\\server\TeamBackups\January"

🏁 Output Structure
JohnDoe_20250115_094233\
│

├── Desktop\

├── Documents\

├── Pictures\

├── OneDrive\

│

├── BackupLog_20250115_094233.txt

└── BackupReport_20250115_094233.pdf

🧱 Limitations

Cannot back up files owned by SYSTEM-only permissions
Very large profiles will take time to enumerate
PDF generation requires Microsoft Edge

🚀 Future Enhancements (Optional Roadmap)

Parallelized copying for faster backups
Incremental backup mode
Email notification system
Intune deployment arguments
GUI wrapper for technicians

✔ Summary

AutomaticBackupScript.ps1 is a reliable, safe, and production-ready tool for user data extraction during IT workflows such as offboarding, device rotation, and troubleshooting.

It demonstrates solid automation engineering practices:

defensive programming
structured logging
resilient error handling
reporting
parameterization
cross-environment compatibility
